WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.620
<v Instructor>Okay, so next we are learning about</v>

2
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.640
step functions.

3
00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.250
And step functions you don't need to know them in depth

4
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.480
going into the exam

5
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.010
but you need to know the capabilities

6
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.190
and where they fit in your solution architecture.

7
00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.870
So the idea with step functions is that you're

8
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.930
going to build serverless visual workflow

9
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:20.250
to help you orchestrate your Lambda functions, but not only.

10
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.610
They represents the flow as adjacent state machine.

11
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.992
And if it has so many features such as sequence actions,

12
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:28.080
parallel actions, conditions, timeouts

13
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.760
error handling, and so on.

14
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.760
The maximum execution time of a workflow is one year.

15
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.780
So that is something you need to know

16
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.070
as a solution architect.

17
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.010
And you have the possibility to implement human

18
00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.010
approval features if you wanted to go on with your flow.

19
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.440
The idea is that if you chain Lambda function

20
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.520
with step functions,

21
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.980
which is a very common pattern, you need to be mindful

22
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.904
that there is going to be latencies between all the calls

23
00:00:49.904 --> 00:00:52.440
to the Lambda functions because you are

24
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.520
passing some information

25
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.700
from one Lambda function to another.

26
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.070
So if we look at step functions visually

27
00:00:59.070 --> 00:00:59.910
here's what it looks like.

28
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:00.990
So this is the graph

29
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.410
that AWS will generate from your JSON state machine.

30
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.028
And as you go through an execution, you will see

31
00:01:07.028 --> 00:01:11.010
in real time what the execution is and how it looks like.

32
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.980
And so you get success, failed, canceled in progress

33
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.210
and then when it's done, you can look

34
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.300
at the entire flow that your step function

35
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.910
went through to go to completion.

36
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.250
So step functions has a lot of integrations.

37
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.410
There are some optimized integrations, for example

38
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.060
with some Lambda functions.

39
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.430
So step functions can obviously invoke Lambda functions.

40
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.470
They can run AWS batch jobs.

41
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.980
They can run ECS tasks and wait for it to complete.

42
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.160
They can also insert an item into a DynamoDB table.

43
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.850
They can even publish messages to SNS and SQS.

44
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.050
They can launch EMR jobs, Glue SageMaker jobs and so on.

45
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.750
They can launch another step functions workflow.

46
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.920
And then using the AWS SDK integration, you get access to

47
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.640
over 200 AWS services directly from your state machine.

48
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.270
And it works like a standard AWS, SDK API call.

49
00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.730
So step functions are very versatile.

50
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.220
On top of it, there are ways to trigger step functions,

51
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.390
and there are multiple ways.

52
00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.560
So you can invoke and trigger a step function state machine

53
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.790
using the management console, using the AWS SDK.

54
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.710
You can use the CLI,

55
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.110
you can use AWS Lambda data will be leveraging

56
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.460
of course the SDK.

57
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.410
You can use API gateway so

58
00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.710
that any call to API gateway does trigger a step function.

59
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:26.730
You can use EventBridge

60
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.250
and then the integrations are limitless.

61
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.927
Code pipelines so it's possible for you

62
00:02:30.927 --> 00:02:34.620
and your CSD pipeline to invent, to invoke step functions.

63
00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:35.453
And finally,

64
00:02:35.453 --> 00:02:38.193
well step functions can also invoke step functions.

65
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.060
Now step function have so many applications

66
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.590
and you can see a bunch

67
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.130
of sample projects at this URL in your console.

68
00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.290
The idea is that there's so many different applications

69
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.580
I can't even count them all

70
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.960
but I'll give you some, right.

71
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.054
Process high volume message from SQS.

72
00:02:54.054 --> 00:02:55.884
and level function to process a lot of message from SQS or

73
00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:58.910
and Lambda function to process a lot of message from SQS.

74
00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.530
Or for example, train a machine learning model when

75
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.530
you have to synchronize between SageMaker, Lambda

76
00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.030
and Amazon S3.

77
00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.651
Or manage a batch job if you want to have

78
00:03:07.651 --> 00:03:10.590
batch Amazon SNS and so on.

79
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.120
Or if you want to manage a container task,

80
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.360
so you wanna run Fargate task whenever something happens.

81
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.370
And so there's so many different of those.

82
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.420
But the idea is that as soon

83
00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.440
as you need to change stuff and orchestrate stuff in AWS

84
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.130
the best way to do so is usually with step functions.

85
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.020
So what can a step function do?

86
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:28.920
It can do many things

87
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.080
and you have to remember them going into the exam.

88
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.540
Number one is do stuff with AWS Lambda,

89
00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.010
they're called Lambda tasks

90
00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.680
and they will just invoke a Lambda function.

91
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.450
You also have activity task

92
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.790
and the activity is something a bit more broad.

93
00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.610
It is you have to set up an HJTTP activity worker.

94
00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:45.840
It could be an EC2 instance,

95
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.840
a mobile device or even an on-premise data center.

96
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.390
And they will pull the step function service

97
00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.480
and they will get the activities out of the step function.

98
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.650
So this is not serverless.

99
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.993
This is your activity worker who have to

100
00:03:57.993 --> 00:04:00.287
pull the step function service and say

101
00:04:00.287 --> 00:04:02.310
Is there something for me to do?

102
00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.100
And then step function, you say, yes there is, do that.

103
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.050
You have service task which is more integrated.

104
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.210
So when step functions will connect

105
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.360
to a supported AWS service and that it can include

106
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.957
a Lambda function an ECS task,

107
00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:17.460
a Fargate container, a DynamoDB table, a batch job

108
00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.320
and SNS topic and SQS queue.

109
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.420
So there is direct integration between step function

110
00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.810
and all these services as part of your workflow.

111
00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.420
And finally, a wait task. If you want to wait

112
00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.720
for a certain duration or until a timestamp.

113
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.780
So a very common question at the exam is going to be

114
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.290
does step functions integrate with AWS mechanical Turk?

115
00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.550
The answer is no.

116
00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.330
The best way to do it is to use SWF

117
00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.570
as you'll see in the next lecture.

118
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.720
So that is the one little thing, if you see Mechanical Turk

119
00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.340
usually they want you to use SWF instead of STEM functions.

120
00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.260
So the standard workflow was the first workflow

121
00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.093
for step functions.

122
00:04:53.093 --> 00:04:55.440
The maximum duration was one year

123
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.460
and you can start about 2000 standard workflow per seconds

124
00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.490
or you can have about 4,000 state transitions

125
00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.290
per second, per account.

126
00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.230
The pricing is going to be per state transition.

127
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.090
And so state transition means whenever

128
00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.820
your execution goes one step at a time.

129
00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.040
You can see the execution history

130
00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.010
and you get exactly once workflow execution.

131
00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.770
So this was the first one, but then some customers

132
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.670
of AWS thought they were very expensive at a high rate

133
00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.520
and sometimes they didn't need all the niceness of one year.

134
00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.270
They just wanted to do very, very quick activities.

135
00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.570
And so AWS came up with the express workflows.

136
00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.820
So the express workflows have a maximum duration

137
00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:36.653
of five minutes.

138
00:05:36.653 --> 00:05:39.750
So they're expected to complete very quickly

139
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.360
but you have a much higher rate of starting them.

140
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.500
You have over 100,000 per second express workflow

141
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.700
you can start.

142
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.400
The state transitions is nearly unlimited

143
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.660
and you're going to be to be priced

144
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.210
by the number of executions you run

145
00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.750
as well as the duration and how much memory you consume

146
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.150
which looks a lot like Lambda functions.

147
00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.000
And the executions themself don't have a history

148
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.120
but they can be inspected using CloudWatch logs

149
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.010
by logging your whatever happens

150
00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.320
in your state machine directly into CloudWatch logs.

151
00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.050
And you get at least once workflow execution.

152
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.760
So just knowing at a high level

153
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.500
the difference I think is pretty obvious when it's

154
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:20.190
for very express, fast high throughput workflows.

155
00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.860
The other one is for longer, slower throughputs.

156
00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.900
It's going to be enough for you going to the exam.

157
00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.960
So in the case of express workflows, we have two cases

158
00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.030
the synchronous and the asynchronous case.

159
00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.120
So if you have a synchronous express workflow

160
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:33.953
you're going to wait

161
00:06:33.953 --> 00:06:36.630
until the workflow completes and then return the results.

162
00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:37.560
This could be very helpful

163
00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.159
if you want to orchestrate microservices, handle errors

164
00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:42.750
retries, have parallel tasks and so on.

165
00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.850
So here's an example with the API gateway.

166
00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.070
We do a request to the API gateway

167
00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.690
which is going to start a workflow

168
00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.561
but synchronously, therefore, we're going to wait

169
00:06:51.561 --> 00:06:55.500
for it to be over to complete its execution.

170
00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.500
The result is going to be returned to the API gateway

171
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.980
which is going to return the results to our users.

172
00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.560
For the asynchronous express workflow is going to

173
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.393
be slightly different.

174
00:07:05.393 --> 00:07:07.710
So we don't wait for the workflow to complete.

175
00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.543
And so this is

176
00:07:08.543 --> 00:07:11.670
for workflows that just don't require immediate responses

177
00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.200
or messaging and so on.

178
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.070
So here's an example.

179
00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.300
We have, again, the API gateway

180
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.070
but this time it's integrated

181
00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.780
with an asynchronous express workflow.

182
00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:22.350
So when the API gateway invokes our workflow

183
00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.000
it's just going to start it.

184
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.440
And then the response is going to be

185
00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.270
that the start has been confirmed

186
00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.850
and then the user knows that the workflow has started

187
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.130
but cannot know the execution result

188
00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.590
at least from this request.

189
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.710
So how do you do error handling in step functions?

190
00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:41.100
Well, we can add retries and end all the errors

191
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.100
from within the state machine and then outside of it

192
00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.800
we can set up Event Bridge to get alerts in case

193
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.510
our state machines fail.

194
00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:49.370
So here's an example.

195
00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.870
The user is going to invoke a state machine

196
00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.940
which is going to invoke Lambda function.

197
00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.860
And let's say that we know that this Lambda function

198
00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.650
can fail from time to time, but we can retry it.

199
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.277
Therefore, we're going to add a retry block saying, Okay

200
00:08:01.277 --> 00:08:05.190
for two more times you're going to retry calling

201
00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:06.450
this Lambda function.

202
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.490
But what if it fails all the time?

203
00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.098
Then we can set up Event Bridge

204
00:08:10.098 --> 00:08:12.309
and we can have a rule to look

205
00:08:12.309 --> 00:08:15.630
for the execution failure events.

206
00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.410
If so, we can trigger an SNS topic.

207
00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.900
So we send this message, this event

208
00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.620
into an SNS topic that is configured to send us an email.

209
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.420
That is sort of the best practices

210
00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.420
for error handling in step functions.

211
00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.330
So let's see a solution architecture with step functions.

212
00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.290
So we have step functions

213
00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.300
and we can invoke them in three different ways.

214
00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.270
We can use, for example, the SDK and the CLI.

215
00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.000
We can use Event Bridge integration to have pretty

216
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.360
much any service trigger a step function workflow.

217
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:46.890
Or we can also integrate it

218
00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.100
with the API gateway through a service proxy integration.

219
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.610
So that's any user doing an HTTP rest API call

220
00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.460
can start a function function, for examples

221
00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.880
as we saw asynchronously or synchronously.

222
00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:00.990
Now within step function

223
00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:03.630
you describe it with adjacent document

224
00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.090
and it gives you a visual representation if you wanted to.

225
00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.650
And you could integrate it, for example

226
00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.230
with different services, such as Lambda

227
00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.050
such as DynamoDB or SQS.

228
00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.630
What I want to show you through this diagram is

229
00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.180
that step functions are not just made

230
00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:19.530
of Lambda functions, okay?

231
00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.140
They have deep integration with other services.

232
00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.570
For example, to read messages from DynamoDB

233
00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.460
or to send messages from SQS.

234
00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.350
We can just do it straight from the step functions.

235
00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.490
We don't need a Lambda function for that.

236
00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:32.850
Okay? So that's it for step functions.

237
00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.913
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

